KNOWNBUG
function_as_statement_return1.sv
--bound 2
^\[main\.p0\] always \(##1 main\.side == 3\): PROVED up to bound 2$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
A function whose body contains `return <value>;` is called as a statement,
discarding the return value (IEEE 1800-2017 13.4.1). The body's side effect
(side = x) must still take effect, so `side` becomes 3 after the first clock
edge. ebmc instead fails during RTL construction with
"return with value requires a function".

Root cause: verilog_rtl_buildert::build_function_call in
src/verilog/verilog_rtl.cpp handles both tasks and functions called as a
statement. It creates a tf_framet without setting tf_frame.return_value
(unlike expand_function_call, which sets it to the function's return symbol).
The ID_return case in build_statement then throws
"return with value requires a function". A function that assigns its result
via `f = ...` (rather than `return`) and is called as a statement works,
confirming the missing return_value in the frame is the cause.
